@cc [owner:spolu,label:security] workspace-isolation
Session filesystem operations, search, subscriptions, and caches MUST be scoped to the authenticated
session's workspace. Matching grant strings or possession of an object ID MUST NOT allow access
across workspaces.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:security] session-authority
Workspace creation MUST require the configured server API key. Session creation MUST verify the
workspace key against durable workspace state; that key may delegate any grant set in its workspace.
Session keys MUST NOT authorize workspace creation, grant administration, or further sessions.
Grants are opaque strings; dfs MUST NOT infer identities or membership from their prefixes.
Session creation MUST reject more than 512 distinct grants. Workspace and grants are fixed for a
session; subsequent requests MUST NOT override them. Expired or closed sessions
MUST be rejected.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:security] workspace-grant-administration
Workspace creation MUST atomically persist its root and caller-supplied explicit root grants.
Session creation MUST NOT attach grants to any object. Grant listing and attachment/revocation MUST
require the workspace key verified against durable state for that workspace; sharing a grant with
an object MUST NOT confer grant administration. Workspace authority may manage grants on any of
its objects, including roots with no grants.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:security] live-grant-authorization
An object's effective grants are the union of its explicit grants and its current ancestors' grants.
Every server session access MUST require an intersection with the session's grants, including access
through a URI, virtual alias, cached content, or search result. Server caches MUST NOT bypass
revocations. Client-side cached authorization may be stale for at most one second.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:backend] atomic-grant-indexes
Explicit grant attachments MUST be indexed both by object and by grant within the workspace.
Update both directions atomically with the mutation: directly in SlateDB for the initial synchronous
path, and first in the server overlay then in SlateDB for the later cached path. Do not materialize
inherited grants in these indexes.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:product] stable-object-identity
Files and directories MUST have server-generated UUIDv4 IDs, MIME types, and xattrs. Moves and
renames MUST preserve object IDs; new content versions MUST receive separate, fresh UUIDv4 IDs.
Store UUIDs as 16 bytes internally. Never deliberately reuse object or version IDs after recovery.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:product] path-independent-uris
Accept dfs://<uuid> and dfs://<name>--<uuid>, with UUIDs encoded as 32 lowercase hexadecimal
characters. Only the UUID identifies the object; the optional name MUST be ignored during
resolution. URI resolution MUST enforce the same workspace and grant checks as path access.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:security] virtual-alias-boundaries
Virtual mounts and /shared entries MUST reference existing objects without granting additional
access. Synthetic parents and mount entries cannot be mutated by filesystem operations. Traversing
.. MUST follow virtual parents without exposing the target's otherwise inaccessible ancestors.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:product] shared-root-discovery
Discover shared roots through the session's grant-to-object index prefixes, reading SlateDB and
merging overlay state when caching is implemented. Deduplicate and paginate results; 512 limits
grant fan-out, not object count. Omit objects already reachable through an authorized ancestor or
workspace-root entry. Every direct /shared entry MUST use <basename>--<object ID>, preserving the full
ID and truncating only the basename at a UTF-8 boundary to fit 255 bytes. Canonical entries inside
shared directories retain their ordinary names. Reserve /shared in the session namespace; a persisted
workspace-root child named shared MUST remain discoverable inside the synthetic folder.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:concurrency] single-shard-owner
The PoC assumes exactly one server process serving one shard; overlapping server instances are
outside its supported scope. Concurrent writes to the same file MUST serialize; writes to unrelated
files MUST NOT wait for that file's writer. Multi-server ownership and fencing are future work.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:performance] fsync-means-visibility
Before acknowledging fsync, the server MUST publish metadata and related indexes atomically and
make the referenced content readable by other clients through the server, modulo client caches.
The initial synchronous path MUST finish required GCS uploads and durable SlateDB batches before
acknowledging mutations. Once local staging is implemented, its foreground path MUST stage content
in server RAM/local disk and MUST NOT wait for GCS uploads, SlateDB durability, or local-disk fsync.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:architecture] content-outside-slatedb
All file contents, including small files, MUST be stored outside SlateDB as immutable versions in
GCS. Upload synchronously in the initial path; stage in server RAM/local disk and upload
asynchronously in the later cached path. SlateDB stores metadata and indexes, never file bytes.
Never evict unuploaded staged content needed by current or pending references; apply backpressure
when staging is full. Coalescing may discard superseded versions after atomic persistence of the
selected prefix, while read snapshots and streams keep their own version pins.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:backend] consistent-durable-prefix
Pending references MUST remain outside SlateDB until their blobs exist in GCS. Persist ordered,
atomic metadata batches forming a consistent prefix of mutations, including namespace and grants.
Recovery MUST use the durable prefix and discard pending local state. Losing recent acknowledged
writes is allowed; dangling content references or partially applied mutations are not. Blob
reclamation MUST preserve contents referenced by live or recoverable state.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:backend;performance] cold-server-start
Every server start MUST begin with empty local metadata and content caches. Discard prior disk cache
data before opening those caches; fail startup if resetting them fails. Recover durable state only
from GCS. Local caches may populate during the running process but MUST NOT survive as reusable
state for the next start.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:security] cache-scope
Content cache identity MUST include workspace, object ID, and content version; a cache owned by one
fixed session may supply workspace scope implicitly. Directory views MUST be scoped to the session,
including grants and virtual mounts. Revalidate current metadata and grants
before the server serves cached content. Recreating a session MUST discard its cached namespace
views.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:product] bidirectional-change-notifications
Product and filesystem clients MUST use the same mutation API and receive change notifications for
cache invalidation/refetch. After a disconnect, missed events, or owner restart, clients MUST
resynchronize rather than assume their cached state is current.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:security] search-authorization
Search MUST prefilter by the session's grants and recheck each hit's existence, content version,
and effective grants against live owner state before exposing results or snippets. Indexed grants
MUST include inheritance; grant changes and moves MUST trigger descendant index updates.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:backend] replayable-search-indexing
Indexing events MUST be recorded atomically with persisted metadata. Consume only durable events,
apply ordered idempotent updates, and checkpoint only after the search index commits. Indexing
MUST NOT block filesystem writes or fsync; search MUST be rebuildable from persisted state.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:rust] propagate-rust-errors
Rust code MUST propagate or explicitly handle fallible operations instead of calling unwrap() or
expect().

@cc [owner:spolu,label:product] file-handle-semantics
Bind file handles to one session, stable object ID, and access mode. Opening
MUST NOT hold a writer lock. Serialize content mutations per file; append MUST choose EOF within
that serialization, and truncate/extension MUST publish a fresh version with zero-filled growth.
O_TRUNC MUST execute as a write during open; read-only handles MUST NOT mutate content.
Every content operation MUST recheck current access; release may discard an inaccessible handle.
Rename preserves handles; the initial PoC rejects
further operations after unlink/replacement deletes their object. Release, session closure/expiry,
and restart invalidate handles. Fsync MUST wait for preceding handle writes and report failures,
rejecting sequence numbers the server has not accepted.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:backend] upload-is-not-publication
Content transfer completion MUST contain no namespace side effects and MUST NOT acknowledge
write/fsync. Explicit upload publication is a separate mutation.
Metadata commits MUST accept internal completed-upload descriptors instead of file bytes, validate
workspace/object/version/size and storage origin, then publish atomically. Synchronous mode MUST
await durability; cached mode MUST acknowledge server visibility and persist asynchronously.
Clients MUST NOT be able to construct trusted descriptors. Publication MUST reauthorize and check
current namespace/content preconditions even when the upload previously passed authorization.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:backend] recoverable-file-mutations
File mutations MUST atomically publish a workspace-scoped request fingerprint and result with their
metadata/index/event batch, first in the overlay in cached mode. Persistence MUST include the receipt
in the same durable batch. Identical retries MUST return the original visible result without
repeating the mutation; changed arguments or bytes MUST conflict. Recovery/status MUST authorize
the current object. A missing receipt or disconnected caller MUST NOT be treated as proof of failure.
Never reclaim receipts until a retry-expiry protocol makes reuse safe. Receipts record original
results, not historical content availability. Coalescing MAY omit intermediate blobs only when the
atomic durable batch preserves its final content references, all receipts/events, and active reads.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:security] fuse-session-and-cache-boundary
A FUSE mount MUST retain its original session scope. Session expiry, closure, or server restart MUST
fail closed within the one-second cached-authorization bound; a new session requires a fresh mount
and namespace state. Client metadata caches MUST require fresh revision checks and discard metadata
on check failure, expiry, or revision change. Immutable content blocks may remain cached, but access
MUST select their version through authorized metadata. Writes and closure MUST NOT wait for client
invalidation. Kernel metadata TTLs MUST share the client freshness deadline, captured before reading
metadata, rather than extend it. Use direct I/O without writeback. Store credentials
only in authorization headers; never log them or embed them in URLs.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:backend] fuse-publication-errors
FUSE writes, flush, and fsync MUST NOT acknowledge client-only bytes. Serialize per-handle sequences
and keep unresolved write errors sticky through flush/fsync. Retry ambiguous edits only with identical
request IDs, arguments, and bytes; do not blindly retry namespace mutations. Unsupported operations,
including cross-mount advisory locks, MUST return errors rather than promise unsupported semantics.
